      "deletion_test": "Removing “does backups right and” preserves the introduction to the design principles without losing a concrete capability or condition.",
      "diagnosis": "“Does backups right” adds an undefined endorsement without explaining what correctness means.",
      "evidence": "The following bullets supply the specific design goals: ease of use, speed, verifiability, security, and efficiency.",
      "line": 58,
      "quote": "does backups right and",
      "source_function": "promotion"
    },
    {
      "deletion_test": "Deleting the word would lose the reader action; preserve the meaning and correct the verb form.",
      "diagnosis": "“Backup” is used as a verb here, but the verb is conventionally written as two words.",
      "evidence": "The phrase “you can backup your files” requires a verb after “can.” Elsewhere, the page uses “backup” as a noun.",
      "line": 67,
      "quote": "you can backup your files",
      "source_function": "supported capability"
    },
    {
      "deletion_test": "Deleting the location clause would lose the subject of the trust assumption. Preserve the qualification and repair its grammatical connection.",
      "diagnosis": "The relative clause lacks the locative connector needed to relate the stored data to its location.",
      "evidence": "Lines 77–78 clarify that the location is an untrusted environment, such as shared storage accessible to administrators.",
      "line": 76,
      "quote": "The location the backup data is stored is",
